1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,090
A robot arm must place its gripper precisely, stay
inside its joint limits, and never collide with itself.

2
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:15,712
This is STP Team 06's collision-aware neural inverse
kinematics, demonstrated on a simulated xArm6.

3
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:20,625
Our project brief has two levels.

4
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:26,151
Level one: a kinematics-aware model that computes joint
angles without self-collision.

5
00:00:26,381 --> 00:00:32,237
Level two: a lightweight reinforcement-learning agent
that re-routes the arm in real time.

6
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:36,127
Forward kinematics is the easy direction.

7
00:00:36,357 --> 00:00:39,299
Each joint rotation is chained onto the next,

8
00:00:39,319 --> 00:00:43,012
and PyBullet returns one exact pose for the gripper: a
position and an orientation.

9
00:00:51,587 --> 00:00:57,096
Inverse kinematics runs backwards, from a target pose to
the joint angles, and it is harder.

10
00:00:57,326 --> 00:01:03,074
For this target, our solver found six different joint
configurations that reach it exactly.

11
00:01:03,304 --> 00:01:06,978
The arm's current joints, called the seed, decide which
one we want.

12
00:01:11,587 --> 00:01:13,779
We generate the training data ourselves.

13
00:01:14,009 --> 00:01:18,150
The generator samples random joint angles: seventy
percent uniformly,

14
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:20,911
and thirty percent close to the joint limits, where
self-collisions concentrate.

15
00:01:23,375 --> 00:01:27,714
PyBullet computes each pose and checks every pair of
links for contact.

16
00:01:27,944 --> 00:01:33,215
Our main dataset has two hundred thousand samples;
sixteen percent are self-colliding.

17
00:01:38,587 --> 00:01:42,087
The network reads thirteen numbers: the target position,

18
00:01:42,107 --> 00:01:44,064
its orientation as a quaternion, and the six seed
angles.

19
00:01:46,070 --> 00:01:51,193
Three hidden layers turn them into twelve outputs, the
sine and cosine of every joint.

20
00:01:51,423 --> 00:01:55,229
The atan2 function turns each pair back into a joint
angle.

21
00:01:55,459 --> 00:02:01,170
Why sine and cosine? On a circle, plus and minus one
hundred and eighty degrees are neighbours,

22
00:02:01,190 --> 00:02:02,312
so the training targets have no artificial jump.

23
00:02:09,587 --> 00:02:14,379
A small classifier learns to predict self-collision from
the joint angles alone;

24
00:02:14,399 --> 00:02:16,260
on held-out poses, it is right ninety-eight percent of
the time.

25
00:02:18,629 --> 00:02:21,848
During training, its probability is added to the loss,

26
00:02:21,868 --> 00:02:23,911
which pushes predictions away from colliding
configurations.

27
00:02:29,587 --> 00:02:34,194
Training uses the Adam optimiser and a fifteen percent
validation split.

28
00:02:34,424 --> 00:02:38,964
The baseline network levels off at about one hundred and
fifteen millimetres;

29
00:02:38,984 --> 00:02:41,548
a deeper residual network, trained on ten times more
data, reaches twenty-three.

30
00:02:48,587 --> 00:02:54,921
At run time, the network answers in about a millisecond,
but on its own it still misses by centimetres.

31
00:02:55,151 --> 00:03:01,131
Its answer becomes the starting point for a
Levenberg–Marquardt solver, which removes the remaining
error.

32
00:03:01,361 --> 00:03:06,910
PyBullet then checks the result independently: pose
error, joint limits and self-collision.

33
00:03:07,140 --> 00:03:10,097
Only an answer that passes every check is accepted.

34
00:03:17,587 --> 00:03:23,758
We compared the methods on the same two thousand held-
out targets, with the arm starting from its home pose.

35
00:03:23,988 --> 00:03:27,012
The network alone never came within one millimetre.

36
00:03:27,032 --> 00:03:29,835
PyBullet's classical solver found a valid answer within
one

37
00:03:29,855 --> 00:03:32,083
millimetre and one degree for under three percent of
targets;

38
00:03:32,103 --> 00:03:34,670
nearly a quarter of its answers collided, and thirty-
nine percent broke a joint limit.

39
00:03:39,707 --> 00:03:44,758
Network plus refinement reached every target within one
millimetre and one degree,

40
00:03:44,778 --> 00:03:45,843
with no collisions and no limit violations.

41
00:03:47,817 --> 00:03:50,541
To be fair, the solver alone also gets there;

42
00:03:50,561 --> 00:03:53,780
the network makes it about a quarter faster and cuts
random restarts threefold.

43
00:03:58,587 --> 00:04:03,849
Level one is complete and reproducible: robot
configuration, data generation,

44
00:04:03,869 --> 00:04:06,108
collision classifier, network training, refinement and
evaluation.

45
00:04:08,776 --> 00:04:13,704
Moving to the real space manipulator means adding one
entry to the robot registry.

46
00:04:15,587 --> 00:04:17,808
Level two is implemented as well:

47
00:04:17,828 --> 00:04:22,767
a PPO agent with eleven thousand parameters corrects a
potential-field controller in real time.

48
00:04:24,661 --> 00:04:30,303
It learns in simulation, rewarded for progress and
penalised for getting close to obstacles.

49
00:04:30,533 --> 00:04:34,838
Here, an obstacle appears and a joint slips: the
straight path collides,

50
00:04:34,858 --> 00:04:36,239
the potential field crawls, and the agent arrives.

51
00:04:38,242 --> 00:04:41,554
Over two hundred unseen episodes of the hardest
scenario,

52
00:04:41,574 --> 00:04:43,932
it reached eighty-four percent of goals, with two
percent collisions.

53
00:04:48,587 --> 00:04:54,474
Fast neural guesses, exact refinement, independent
checks, and a learned controller on top.

54
00:04:54,704 --> 00:04:57,175
Thank you from STP Team 06.
